ame, oldval)` where oldval = the original value obtained via getattr. On undo, it does `setattr(target, name, oldval)` if the attribute existed, or `delattr` if it didn't exist... Hmm, actually pytest's monkeypatch: \n\n```python\ndef setattr(self, target, name, value=notset, raising=True):\n    ...\n    oldval = getattr(target, name, notset)\n    if raising and oldval is notset:\n        raise AttributeError(...)\n    self._setitem.append((target, name, oldval))\n    setattr(target, name, value)\n```\n\nAnd undo:\n\n```python\ndef _setitem_undo(self, obj, name, value):\n    if value is not notset:\n        setattr(obj, name, value)\n    else:\n        delattr(obj, name)\n```\n\nSo if `eightbit_call` didn't exist as an instance attribute (oldval via getattr returns the bound method \u2014 wait, getattr(fg, 'eightbit_call') returns the bound method, which is not notset). So oldval = bound method, and undo does setattr(fg, 'eightbit_call', bound_method) \u2014 which sets an instance attribute equal to the bound method. That still works functionally (the bound method is the same), though it leaves an instance attribute behind. Functionally equivalent, no pollution in behavior. Hmm, but it does technically leave state. Actually wait, let me re-check pytest's monkeypatch implementation:\n\n```python\n@contextmanager\ndef context(self):\n    ...\n\ndef setattr(self, target, name, value=notset, raising=True):\n    \"\"\"Set attribute value on target, memorize the old value.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(target, str):\n        target = resolve(target)\n    if value is notset:\n        if isinstance(name, str):\n            raise TypeError(...)\n        ...\n    oldval = getattr(target, name, notset)\n    if raising and oldval is notset:\n        raise AttributeError(f\"{target!r} has no attribute {name!r}\")\n\n    # avoid class descriptors like staticmethod/classmethod\n    if inspect.isclass(target):\n        oldval = target.__dict__.get(name, notset)\n    self._setitem.append((target, name, oldval))\n    setattr(target, name, value)\n```\n\nAnd:\n\n```python\ndef _setitem_undo(self, obj, name, value):\n    if value is not notset:\n        setattr(obj, name, value)\n    else:\n        delattr(obj, name)\n```\n\nSo for an instance target, oldval = getattr(fg, 'eightbit_call') = bound method (not notset). Undo: setattr(fg, 'eightbit_call', bound_method). This sets an instance attribute. Functionally identical behavior, but leaves `fg.__dict__['eightbit_call']` set. Is that \"state pollution\"? Functionally no \u2014 the bound method behaves identically. But to be extra clean, I could avoid monkeypatching instance methods and instead subclass or create a fresh Register.\n\nAlternative cleaner approach: create a fresh Register instance for each test using the constructor. Then no module-level state is touched at all. But I need the constructor signature. Let me check with get_info.\n\nActually, even better: I can create a fresh instance and monkeypatch on that fresh instance \u2014 pollution on a throwaway object doesn't matter.\n\nBut creating a fresh Register requires knowing the constructor args (val, rules, escape_seq) and the rules module. Let me check get_info for 'Register.__init__' or 'base.Register'.\n\nHmm, but there's a risk: the get_info tool might only know about symbols in the file being tested (sty/primitive.py). Let me try a broad set of queries and see what comes back.\n\nPlan for get_info calls (all independent, one block):\n- 'Register'\n- 'Register.__init__'\n- 'Register.__call__'\n- 'Register.eightbit_call'\n- 'Register.rgb_call'\n- 'Register.is_muted'\n- 'Register.mute'\n- 'Register.unmute'\n- 'fg'\n- 'render'\n- 'rules'\n\nThat's a lot of calls but they're all independent. Let me do it.\n\nAfter getting the info, I'll write the test.\n\nLet me draft the test file now, and refine after get_info:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for sty.primitive.Register.__call__ (lines 99-120).\"\"\"\n\nimport pytest\n\nimport sty\nfrom sty i